Push it back off of there, in the short term. I got myself worked up 
about it. Might play with it on my VIC-20 or C64; and then kick the 
results your way. (Looking at the code again -- yes, I'm able to make 
it do a list-able ASCII version, thanks to a conversion program for 
the PC that I think was called CBM2ASC, or something like that). At 
the very least, some of the POKEs and such need to be explained. Any 
comments that might have existed in the original source code, were 
deleted by the mag, to save space. I could at least add some REMs.

> So, experts out there, IS it possible to
> redefine the character set in BASIC?

Or is is possible to intermix whatever flavor of BASIC Adam would be 
using, with machine code? The Commodore machines let you POKE things 
in from a list of DATA statements: putting machine language routines 
into some unused area of the machine, then temporarily transferring 
control to that ML routine; then going back to BASIC. Do-able here?

> Would [text adventures] be "simple"
> or would they be fun.

Probably pretty lame, actually ... or so my memory says, anyway. It 
would be simple to do; but probably not much more than a curiousity.

Random idea: I'm thinking of a game idea, with a possible working 
title of Red Shirt; or maybe Space Gladiator. It would be roughly the 
equivalent of a single room, from a dungeon type of game.

The idea would be a sick nod towards the early Star Trek shows: if 
the Captain ever said, "Ensign, you're with me" right before an away 
mission, to a person the audience had never seen before, it was 
pretty much guaranteed that person was dead meat two seconds after 
they beamed down to some new planet. (A movie spoofed the idea: 
Galaxy Trek or some such, if I recall?) So, the game idea would be a 
matter of being teleported to some place; you fight whatever's there; 
you die some utterly random and probably horrible death; and then 
maybe the other guys -- the important people -- will teleport in ... 
if they decide that you've made the place safe enough. If not, then I 
guess you get a memorial plaque displayed in the lower sub-basement 
of the ship's storage areas or the kitchens or Mess Hall; instead of 
displayed in the ship's hallways; or (best case) up on the Bridge. So 
the game would break down to one main (quick!) game; plus a standard 
intro; plus a few different background walls, for your name plaque?

Re: Sword of Fargoal -- I'll dig around in my collection of discs and 
such. I probably have the C64 version, somewhere; and maybe the VIC-
20 version. I did make a start at having mapped out memory usage, 
etc.: finding the few main ML routines, and listed the BASIC parts.

Re: Beneath Apple Manor -- that's mostly a personal dream thing. I'd 
love to convert that great old dungeon game to one or more old game 
consoles of computer formats; but no BASIC source code has ever been 
available, that I'm aware of. (Rumor has it the original was written 
in Integer Basic on the Apple; then compiled using the Galfo BASIC 
compiler.) It was later ported by the author to the Atari 8-bit, and 
to the PC. You can freely download the PC version (direct link):

http://worth. bol.ucla. edu/applemanor. ZIP

Don Worth, the program's author, runs this page about himself:

http://worth. bol.ucla. edu/

To me at least, it's pretty interesting stuff. Oh, and he has an 
interest in history ... maybe I'll e-mail him, begging for source?

> Good ideas, Ward.  You're thinking within the constrains of BASIC.

That's me ... a "basic" kinda guy!  ;-)

-- Ward Shrake --




